  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will develop two different methods to factor special polynomials. While these polynomials can be factored using the methods from the previous lesson, these special cases allow for a more streamlined approach to factoring. One important skill that you will develop is the ability to recognize when you have a polynomial that fits into these special cases.</para>
<para>When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list>
  <item>
    Factor perfect square trinomials.
  </item>
  <item>
    Factor differences of squares.
  </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list>
  <item>
    6.6.1: Identifying Perfect Square Trinomials
  </item>
  <item>
    6.6.2: Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials
  </item>
  <list>
    <item>
      6.6.2: Self Check
    </item>
    <item>6.6.2: Additional Resources</item>
  </list>
  <item>
    6.6.3: Factoring the Difference of Squares
  </item>
  <list>
    <item>
      6.6.3: Self Check

    </item>
    <item>6.6.3: Additional Resources</item>
  </list>
  <item>
    6.6.4: Sorting Polynomials by Factoring Methods
  </item>
</list>
<para>After that, you’ll <emphasis>practice and review</emphasis>.</para>
<list>
  <item>
    6.6.5: Practice
  </item>
  <item>
    6.6.6: Lesson Summary
  </item>
</list></section>
